      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T02:33:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Once return from this hypercall, the host will switch to use EPT, which doesn\u0027t have IOMMU MMIO map already. So if there is any chance for the host IOMMU IRQ_REMAP driver (which is enabled much earlier than pkvm deprivilege, right?) to access the IOMMU MMIO via memory map before enabling the pkvm_enabled_key but after this hypercall, it will cause EPT_VIOLATION to the pkvm, which I think the pkvm hypervisor doesn\u0027t handle this case. So probably we should enable the pkvm_enabled_key in this function with IRQ disabled so that, at lease on this CPU, there is no chance for the host to use memory map to access the IOMMU MMIO. For the other CPUs, they are not finalized yet which means they are not using EPT yet. And the pkvm_enabled_key is not percpu, so for the other CPUs, they will also see the pkvm_enabled_key to be true in this case and switch to use hypercall (pv method), right? Is there any impact to the IRQ_REMAP driver?",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "27b9e65b_5540c1ff",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T07:38:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, IIUC it is essentially the same question I asked in https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/3590991/comments/64e04893_f0eaf13d . If we are sure that IRQ_REMAP\u0027s access to the IOMMU MMIO may only happen during early APIC setup or, for example, during suppend/resume, not in parallel with pKVM initialization, then it seems there is no issue (and then we don\u0027t need to disable IRQs, right?).\n\nOtherwise, if it may happen in parallel with CPUs deprivileging, then yes, we should disable IRQs (in case such MMIO access may happen in an IRQ handler), but more importantly, we need to restore the percpu variables (since we are back to the problem which was the reason for introducing those percpu variables in the first place)?",
      "parentUuid": "a17cfd47_0cce4d69",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2133efe1_948aa5c7",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T08:11:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "By looking the irq_remapping.c, looks like it doesn\u0027t use dmar_read/write in parallel with pkvm but it also seems like there is no guarantee that this won\u0027t be happened in future. So probably using the percpu variables may be safer and yes, that is also the reason to use percpu variable in the first place. Alternative, extending the instruction decoder in the pkvm hypervisor to support decoding mov instruction with src/dest different registers (currently only support rax for pci configuration space accessing) if the effort is not too much, which can reduce the code change to the IOMMU read/write wrapper. Of cause, this alternative is more complex than just using a percpu variable, and we can discuss this later when preparing the upstream RFC.",
      "parentUuid": "27b9e65b_5540c1ff",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ab21eeb9_e4df4eac",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T08:25:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "IMHO, a single static key is simpler and more efficient than percpu variables, so it feels a bit excessive to use percpu variables to protect against something that may only hypothetically (and rather unlikely) happen in future kernels.\n\nPerhaps we could just add some WARN_ON() to detect this condition if it ever happens (although I\u0027m not sure where and how we would add it)?",
      "parentUuid": "2133efe1_948aa5c7",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "251c65cf_edeaa147",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T08:54:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes. It seems not easy to add this WARN_ON(). BTW, enabling pkvm_enabled_key can still be done in an irq disabled context, right after the finalise hypercall, so that the host has no chance to access the IOMMU MMIO via memory mapped way after the finalise on this CPU. This is something the pkvm code can guarantee, right? For the other CPUs, probably can add some comments to mention the other CPUs may still use memory mapped way to access the IOMMU MMIO until they are deprivileged.",
      "parentUuid": "ab21eeb9_e4df4eac",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "db08df96_381d2864",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 1041,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T10:21:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, now I see what you mean: add local_irq_disable()/local_irq_enable() around the entire `pkvm_init_finalise()` + `static_branch_enable(\u0026pkvm_enabled_key)` in vmx_pkvm_init(), not just around this_cpu_do_finalise_hc()? (Or rather move static_branch_enable(\u0026pkvm_enabled_key) into pkvm_init_finalise(), so that we can do local_irq_disable()/local_irq_enable() together with get_cpu()/put_cpu(), and set pkvm_enabled_key before enabling IRQs _and_ putting the CPU.) And thereby guarantee that each CPU, including the first CPU, at each moment in time is in one of 3 possible states:\n\n1. deprivileging not started yet (so the host can safely access physical MMIO)\n2. deprivileging started and it is guaranteed that it will not be interrupted (thanks to local_irq_disable()/local_irq_enable() if it\u0027s the first CPU, or to the IPI interrupt context if it\u0027s some other CPU)\n3. deprivileging finished, pkvm_enabled_key already set, and the host can safely use hypercalls\n\nYeah, this seems reasonable, easy to do and preventing potential problem with IRQ_REMAP (and maybe some other potential subtle issues we don\u0027t know about yet). And it seems that using percpu variables would have no advantage over this solution?",
      "parentUuid": "251c65cf_edeaa147",
      "revId": "c1622880ddfcee6dcb19d17e8d1730eb5dab265a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a79a62e5_c42be642",
